# Python coding style

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic style rules to Python's own
idiom. Applies only to `*.py`/`*.pyi` files (W1-AC8) — everything not
Python-specific still comes from the common rules this file `extends`.

## Formatting and typing

- Format with the project's configured formatter (`ruff format` or `black`);
  do not hand-format around a formatter that is already configured.
- Type-hint every new or touched public function signature (`def f(x: int)
  -> str:`), including `Optional`/`| None` for a parameter or return that can
  be absent. Do not add hints to untouched code in the same change.
- Prefer `from __future__ import annotations` in modules that predate PEP 604
  (`int | None`) rather than importing `Optional`/`Union` piecemeal.
- Use `pathlib.Path`, not `os.path` string joins, for new filesystem code.

## Naming and structure

- `snake_case` for functions, variables and modules; `PascalCase` for
  classes; `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` for module-level constants — the PEP 8
  baseline, not a house variant of it.
- One public class or cohesive function group per module; a module that
  outgrows one screen's worth of unrelated public names should split.
- Private module/class internals get a single leading underscore
  (`_helper`), not name-mangled double-underscore unless subclass
  name-clash protection is the actual intent.

## Errors and imports

- Raise a specific exception type (`ValueError`, `KeyError`, or a project
  exception class), never a bare `except:` or `except Exception:` that
  swallows an unrelated failure — catch the specific type you can handle.
- Absolute imports (`from mypackage.module import thing`), not relative
  dot-imports (`from ..module import thing`), for anything crossing a
  package boundary; relative imports are fine within one package's own
  submodules.
- Group imports stdlib / third-party / local, each group alphabetized, one
  blank line between groups — the convention `isort`/`ruff --select I`
  enforce; run it instead of hand-ordering when the project has it
  configured.

## Docstrings

- Every public function, class and module gets a docstring stating what it
  does and, for a function with non-obvious parameters, what each one means
  — a one-line summary is enough when the signature is already
  self-describing.
- Match the project's existing docstring convention (Google-style, NumPy-style,
  or plain reST) rather than introducing a second style in one file.
